[7] binary-pulsar aberration from the causal record delay (mean field h = G_R F <T>; F(w) = 1 + i w delta + ... in the CMB frame)
    star 2 is attracted to star 1's position a time delta earlier -> tangential force along v -> dE/dt = +(4 mu/M) Omega^2 delta |E| (circular, CM at rest)
    double pulsar J0737-3039A/B: Omega = 7.112e-04 s^-1; GR-consistency 1.3e-04 of Pdot_GR -> delta < 2.4e-14 s; uncompensated F_u (n=3, tau=l/c): l < 2.4e-06 m
      delay-compensated (1 - i n w tau)(1 - i w tau)^-n, n=3: effective delay C3 tau^3 Omega^2 (C3=8) -> tau_c < 1.8e-03 s
      delay-compensated (1 - i n w tau)(1 - i w tau)^-n, n=4: effective delay C3 tau^3 Omega^2 (C3=20) -> tau_c < 1.3e-03 s
    Hulse-Taylor B1913+16: Omega = 2.251e-04 s^-1; GR-consistency 1.6e-03 of Pdot_GR -> delta < 1.8e-12 s; uncompensated F_u (n=3, tau=l/c): l < 1.8e-04 m
      delay-compensated (1 - i n w tau)(1 - i w tau)^-n, n=3: effective delay C3 tau^3 Omega^2 (C3=8) -> tau_c < 1.6e-02 s
      delay-compensated (1 - i n w tau)(1 - i w tau)^-n, n=4: effective delay C3 tau^3 Omega^2 (C3=20) -> tau_c < 1.2e-02 s
    Sun-planet orbits: Omega^2 delta over 1e2 yr with delta = 5e-14 s: Mercury relative energy change = 1.1e-16 (negligible)
    frame-velocity offset of the field centre (R11-5 [6]) is the same delay times W; zero for the compensated gain at first order

