[10] self-drag of a moving body by its own delayed field (new test; Newtonian mean field, uniform motion v along z)
     F_i = -4 pi G int d^3k/(2pi)^3 k_i Im F(k.v) |rho~(k)|^2/k^2 ; first order Im F = delta k.v  =>  F = -(4 pi G/3) delta (int rho^2) v
     tau=1e-03 (v tau/a = 1e-03): uncompensated n=3: Gamma = 2.2448e-05 vs first-order (4piG/3) delta int rho^2/M: 2.2448e-05; compensated n=4: Gamma = 1.347e-10 (ratio 6.0e-06; leading term ~ (v tau/a)^2)
     tau=1e-02 (v tau/a = 1e-02): uncompensated n=3: Gamma = 2.2442e-04 vs first-order (4piG/3) delta int rho^2/M: 2.2448e-04; compensated n=4: Gamma = 1.346e-07 (ratio 6.0e-04; leading term ~ (v tau/a)^2)
     neutron star 1.3 Msun, R=12 km (uniform-density lower bound): (4 pi G/3) rho = 9.98e+07 s^-2
     Sun (mean density): (4 pi G/3) rho = 3.94e-07 s^-2
     Earth: (4 pi G/3) rho = 1.54e-06 s^-2
     double pulsar: Pdot_b/P_b = -3 Gamma; tolerance 1.3e-4 |Pdot_GR|/P_b -> Gamma < 6.1e-21 s^-1 -> delta < 6.2e-29 s -> uncompensated F_u (n=3, tau=l/c): l < 6.1e-21 m
     R11-5 window lower end l = 4e-10 m: delta = 4.0e-18 s -> NS drag rate 4.0e-10 s^-1 (e-fold 79 yr)
     Earth at l = 5e-6 m: drag rate 7.7e-20 s^-1; velocity loss over t_H = 0.03 (relative)
     subtraction: none (the drag is the odd-in-omega part of F; the even part is conservative and is not subtracted, it gives no force on a symmetric body)

[11] compensated gains and the neutron-star drag (Taylor regime, smooth n=1 polytrope)
     Im F(w) = delta w + c3 (w tau)^3 + c5 (w tau)^5 + ... ; drag Gamma = (4 pi G/M) [delta/3 int rho^2 + c3 tau^3 v^2/5 int |grad rho|^2 + c5 tau^5 v^4/7 int |lap rho|^2 + ...]
     uncompensated n=3: Im F coefficients x^1..x^5 = +3.000, -10.000, +21.000
     first moment zero, n=4: Im F coefficients x^1..x^5 = +0.000, +20.000, -84.000
     first+third moments zero, n=5: P(z) = 1 + 5.0000 z + 8.0000 z^2; Im F coefficients x^1, x^3, x^5 = +0.0e+00, +0.0e+00, +56.000
     |F|^2 of that gain: max over w = 2.83; high-w slope d ln|F|^2/d ln w = -6.00 (backaction K ~ |F|^2/w^2 falls faster than w^-6)
     polytrope n=1, M=1.3 Msun, R=12 km: rho_c = 1.17e+18 kg/m^3; int rho^2/M = 5.87e+17; int|grad rho|^2/M = 4.03e+10 m^-2 kg/m^3; int|lap rho|^2/M = 2.76e+03
     uncompensated: delta < 3.7e-29 s (polytrope; uniform-sphere value 6.2e-29 s)
     first moment zero (n=4, c3=+20): tau < 7.9e-12 s (c tau < 2.4e-03 m) at v = 300 km/s
     first+third moments zero (n=5, c5=+56): interior-only estimate tau < 3.3e-08 s (c tau < 1.0e+01 m); NOT a bound: the n=1 surface kink makes int|lap rho|^2 diverge, so the drag is set by surface structure at scale v tau = 1.0e-02 m (suggested only)
     strict family tau = l/c with first moment zero, l = 5e-6 m: Gamma = 5.6e-29 s^-1 (limit 6.1e-21)
     subtraction: none
